=>Modules in Node JS

    ->Modules are a way to organize and encapsulate code into reusable and maintainable components. Each module in Node.js has its own context, so it cannot interfere with other modules unless explicitly stated.

=>Types of Modules
    ->Core Modules: These are modules provided by Node.js itself and can be accessed without any additional installation. Examples include fs (file system), http, path, and url.

    ->Local Modules: These are user-defined modules. They can be created to encapsulate specific functionality and can be imported into other parts of the application.

    ->Third-Party Modules: These are modules created by the Node.js community and can be installed using the Node Package Manager (npm). Examples include express, mongoose, and lodash.
    -------------------------------------------------

=>Creating and Using Modules
    1. Core Modules
    Core modules are included with Node.js and can be used without additional installation.

    ------------------------------------------
    Example: Using the fs Core Module

    // coreModuleExample.js
    const fs = require('fs');

    fs.readFile('example.txt', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
        if (err) throw err;
        console.log(data);
    });

    ----------------------------------------------

    2. Local Modules
    You can create your own modules to organize and reuse code.

    Example: Creating and Using a Local Module

    math.js(Local Module)

    // math.js
    ---------------------------------
    const add = (a, b) => a + b;
    const subtract = (a, b) => a - b;

    module.exports = { add, subtract };
    ----------------------------------

    // app.js
    const math = require('./math');

    const sum = math.add(5, 3);
    const difference = math.subtract(5, 3);

    console.log(`Sum: ${sum}`);
    console.log(`Difference: ${difference}`);

    -----------------------------------
    
    3. Third-Party Modules
    Third-party modules can be installed using npm and then used in your application.

    Example: Using the lodash Third-Party Module
    Install lodash

    ------------------------------------
    npm install lodash
    ------------------------------------

    app.js(Using Third-Party Module)
    // app.js
    --------------------------------------
    const _ = require('lodash');

    const array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    const reversedArray = _.reverse(array.slice());

    console.log('Reversed Array:', reversedArray);
    ---------------------------------------

=>Exporting and Importing Modules
    In Node.js, you can use the module.exports object to export functions, objects, or values from a module, and the require function to import them.

    Example: Exporting and Importing Functions
    greet.js

    -----------------------------------------
    // greet.js
    function greet(name) {
        return `Hello, ${name}!`;
    }

    module.exports = greet;

    -----------------------------------------

    // app.js
    const greet = require('./greet');

    console.log(greet('Alice'));
    --------------------------------------------

    Summary
    ->Core Modules: Built-in modules provided by Node.js(e.g., fs, http).

    ->Local Modules: Custom modules created by the user.

    ->Third-Party Modules: Modules installed using npm (e.g., express, lodash).